Future Tech on modern marbles.

11
00:00:43,320 --> 00:00:45,560
Welcome to the year 2060.

12
00:00:46,120 --> 00:00:51,000
As you'll notice, the world has changed quite dramatically in the past 50 years.

13
00:00:52,080 --> 00:00:53,560
Several things are bigger.

14
00:00:53,561 --> 00:00:58,160
World's tallest skyscraper, one-half mile in height.

15
00:00:58,940 --> 00:01:00,640
But most things are smaller.

16
00:01:01,240 --> 00:01:04,280
Nanotech medicines cure all forms of cancer.

17
00:01:04,760 --> 00:01:05,880
Some things are faster.

18
00:01:06,920 --> 00:01:10,480
Hypersonic flight from New York to Tokyo takes two hours.

19
00:01:11,320 --> 00:01:13,120
While other things last longer.

20
00:01:13,780 --> 00:01:17,520
Average human lifespan, 125 years.

21
00:01:18,520 --> 00:01:20,920
Of course, these are just predictions.

22
00:01:20,921 --> 00:01:23,920
will they ever be reality?

23
00:01:23,921 --> 00:01:28,920
What technologies today will revolutionize tomorrow's personal transportation?

24
00:01:28,921 --> 00:01:35,920
Bionics, warfare, home entertainment, and space exploration.

25
00:01:35,921 --> 00:01:39,920
And why bother even trying to predict what's coming?

26
00:01:39,921 --> 00:01:42,920
Forecasting the future is very important at a couple of levels.

27
00:01:42,921 --> 00:01:45,920
If you can't anticipate what's going to happen,

28
00:01:45,921 --> 00:01:48,920
you're going to be stuck reacting to what's happening.

29
00:01:48,921 --> 00:01:56,680
happening. A good place to start on your road trip into the future is on the road.

30
00:02:01,240 --> 00:02:03,446
What will tomorrow's wheels be like?

31
00:02:03,447 --> 00:02:07,000
How about a vehicle that uses the most abundant element in

32
00:02:07,001 --> 00:02:13,844
the universe as a fuel? Its only emissions are water vapor and a little heat.

33
00:02:13,845 --> 00:02:15,000
It's twice as

34
00:02:15,001 --> 00:02:19,440
efficient as today's combustion engines, and you can refuel the vehicle at home.

35
00:02:19,441 --> 00:02:26,236
No fantasy, just such test vehicles are already on the road.

36
00:02:26,237 --> 00:02:30,540
Get ready for the hydrogen revolution.

37
00:02:30,541 --> 00:02:35,460
The hydrogen fuel cell vehicle is going to do to today's cars and trucks what today's

38
00:02:35,461 --> 00:02:39,039
cars and trucks did to the horse and buggy of a hundred and fifty years ago.

39
00:02:39,040 --> 00:02:39,840
It's a completely

40
00:02:39,841 --> 00:02:43,840
revolutionary approach to automotive transportation.

41
00:02:43,841 --> 00:02:50,840
Right now, nearly every major automobile manufacturer in the world is working on hydrogen fuel cell technology,

42
00:02:50,841 --> 00:02:57,840
the technology that could begin to supplant gas-burning vehicles in the next 15 to 30 years.

43
00:02:57,841 --> 00:03:04,840
A fuel cell vehicle essentially is an electric vehicle, but instead of using a battery to provide the power,

44
00:03:04,841 --> 00:03:08,840
it uses a fuel cell to generate electricity on board.

45
00:03:08,841 --> 00:03:11,140
These are very high-performance cars.

46
00:03:11,420 --> 00:03:17,620
They have great get-up-and-go, wonderful acceleration, and really good driving performance.

47
00:03:20,080 --> 00:03:23,180
This is General Motors' Hydrogen 3 vehicle.

48
00:03:23,700 --> 00:03:28,020
This is not a conventional vehicle, but a fuel cell vehicle powered by hydrogen.

49
00:03:28,380 --> 00:03:33,820
The hydrogen is stored in the back where the gas tank would normally be, and under the hood is a fuel cell.

50
00:03:33,821 --> 00:03:40,140
Now the fuel cell system combines hydrogen and oxygen to make electricity, which powers

51
00:03:40,141 --> 00:03:41,280
the vehicle.

52
00:03:41,281 --> 00:03:45,880
The waste product is water, which goes out the tailpipe, and heat, which is thrown overboard

53
00:03:45,881 --> 00:03:46,880
in the radiator.

54
00:03:46,881 --> 00:03:56,380
A fuel cell will continue to produce electricity as long as oxygen and hydrogen are supplied.

55
00:03:56,381 --> 00:04:00,960
Fuel cells are different from batteries in this respect.

56
00:04:00,961 --> 00:04:07,140
are self-contained and eventually run down if not recharged. A common type of

57
00:04:07,141 --> 00:04:11,700
fuel cell uses a proton exchange membrane which is sandwiched between two

58
00:04:11,701 --> 00:04:19,200
electrodes. Hydrogen gas is fed to one side while oxygen is fed to the other.

59
00:04:19,201 --> 00:04:24,960
The hydrogen atoms are attracted to the oxygen atoms but the membrane only

60
00:04:24,961 --> 00:04:31,400
allows the positively charged hydrogen protons through. The electrons have to

61
00:04:31,401 --> 00:04:36,720
take the long way around. This flow of electrons generates an electrical

62
00:04:36,721 --> 00:04:43,520
current. Although the basic concept of fuel cells dates back to the 1890s, it

63
00:04:43,521 --> 00:04:50,120
wasn't until the 1960s that fuel cell technology really took off.

64
00:04:54,960 --> 00:05:04,420
NASA, looking for an alternative to heavy batteries, used fuel cells on the Gemini missions,

65
00:05:04,760 --> 00:05:10,860
and later on the Moon missions, and eventually on the Space Shuttle missions.

66
00:05:11,760 --> 00:05:16,480
One side benefit, the byproduct of the fuel cell is drinkable water.

67
00:05:16,481 --> 00:05:27,860
but back on the ground this car can go 96 miles an hour and performs like a conventional minivan

68
00:05:27,861 --> 00:05:34,220
it's impressive but engineers still have several technical speed bumps to overcome

69
00:05:34,221 --> 00:05:37,560
before you're kicking the tires of your new fuel cell vehicle

70
00:05:39,560 --> 00:05:45,880
the challenges that confront us right now are on board storage of hydrogen how much hydrogen can

71
00:05:45,881 --> 00:05:48,420
and you store on board a vehicle to give it the range that it needs.

72
00:05:48,740 --> 00:05:52,060
We have to lower component costs so this vehicle is more affordable.

73
00:05:53,040 --> 00:05:56,800
A robust national refueling infrastructure is also extremely critical.

74
00:05:58,160 --> 00:06:03,040
Hydrogen can be produced from diverse sources, including coal and natural gas.

75
00:06:04,560 --> 00:06:07,120
Hydrogen can even be produced by electrolysis,

76
00:06:08,100 --> 00:06:13,720
passing an electric current through water to break its molecules apart into hydrogen and oxygen gas.

77
00:06:15,880 --> 00:06:19,380
It could be what frees the country from dependence on

78
00:06:19,381 --> 00:06:21,380
foreign oil.

79
00:06:21,381 --> 00:06:24,380
But today, hydrogen production isn't as green as it might

80
00:06:24,381 --> 00:06:26,380
seem.

81
00:06:26,381 --> 00:06:30,380
Right now, most commercially produced hydrogen comes from

82
00:06:30,381 --> 00:06:32,380
natural gas.

83
00:06:32,381 --> 00:06:36,380
If fossil fuels are used in the production of hydrogen, the

84
00:06:36,381 --> 00:06:38,380
process generates air pollutants.

85
00:06:38,381 --> 00:06:41,380
We're perhaps no better off.

86
00:06:41,381 --> 00:06:44,080
That's why the goal of many environmental

87
00:06:44,081 --> 00:06:51,680
That's why the goal of many environmentally conscious researchers is eventually to produce hydrogen entirely from renewable sources.

88
00:06:53,880 --> 00:07:02,020
Renewable sources include wind energy, solar energy, geothermal, ways of making electricity.

89
00:07:02,021 --> 00:07:07,300
And of course that electricity then can be used to produce hydrogen from water.

90
00:07:09,200 --> 00:07:13,340
Another challenge is building a hydrogen infrastructure from the ground up.

91
00:07:14,080 --> 00:07:19,080
California Governor Arnold Schwarzenegger believes he has the answer.

92
00:07:19,081 --> 00:07:25,840
I will sign an executive order creating a public and private partnership that will create

93
00:07:25,841 --> 00:07:32,660
hydrogen highways all over the state of California by the year 2010.

94
00:07:32,661 --> 00:07:38,720
Governor Schwarzenegger's plan for a hydrogen highway calls for 150 to 200 hydrogen refueling

95
00:07:38,721 --> 00:07:48,200
stations strategically placed along California's highways.

96
00:07:48,201 --> 00:07:53,840
Fueling stations along major highways will be essential for long distance travel.

97
00:07:53,841 --> 00:08:00,580
But for most in-town commuters, Honda has a plan to make filling up even easier.

98
00:08:00,581 --> 00:08:05,440
As easy as a trip to your garage.

99
00:08:05,441 --> 00:08:10,860
This home energy station connects to a home's natural gas line and is capable of producing

100
00:08:10,861 --> 00:08:15,600
hydrogen for fuel cell vehicles.

101
00:08:15,601 --> 00:08:20,620
A stationary fuel cell could even power the house.

102
00:08:20,621 --> 00:08:25,720
Although there are test vehicles on the road and several infrastructure plans in progress,

103
00:08:25,721 --> 00:08:31,260
it'll still be a while before you can fuel your car with hydrogen.

104
00:08:31,261 --> 00:08:34,680
Fuel cells are still extremely expensive.

105
