00:26:11,721 --> 00:26:14,560
The answer is yes.

372
00:26:14,561 --> 00:26:17,540
And they're already here.

373
00:26:17,541 --> 00:26:24,860
Scientists around the world are currently working on brain-machine interfaces, BMIs.

374
00:26:24,861 --> 00:26:32,520
These devices facilitate direct communication between the brain and the computer.

375
00:26:32,521 --> 00:26:35,760
For the severely disabled, it's a way to control the computer

376
00:26:35,761 --> 00:26:37,260
without using a mouse or keyboard.

377
00:26:41,200 --> 00:26:44,340
Andrew Junker of Brain Actuated Technologies

378
00:26:44,341 --> 00:26:47,600
invented Brain Fingers, which is a BMI that

379
00:26:47,601 --> 00:26:51,800
can be used for communicating, to surf the web,

380
00:26:51,801 --> 00:26:55,120
or to control a computer-equipped house.

381
00:26:55,121 --> 00:26:58,040
It can also control off-the-shelf software.

382
00:26:58,041 --> 00:27:00,580
This is the CyberLink Brain Fingers system.

383
00:27:00,581 --> 00:27:05,260
consists of a headband that plugs into an interface box which goes into the computer.

384
00:27:05,740 --> 00:27:11,840
The way I connect, I wear this headband that has three sensors that will pick up the voltage at my forehead.

385
00:27:16,100 --> 00:27:19,920
Brain cells communicate by producing tiny electrical impulses.

386
00:27:21,440 --> 00:27:24,640
Brain fingers can detect and decode some of these impulses.

387
00:27:25,840 --> 00:27:29,860
The signals are broken into frequencies Andrew calls brain fingers.

388
00:27:30,580 --> 00:27:36,800
So we could use that signal, for example, to control or create a switch.

389
00:27:37,260 --> 00:27:42,820
What's shown in this display is that muscle signal as it moves across the screen.

390
00:27:43,340 --> 00:27:48,860
And in the simplest case, if I raise an eyebrow quickly, I have a switch.

391
00:27:49,260 --> 00:27:51,880
You notice that when I go up, switch comes on.

392
00:27:52,420 --> 00:27:53,600
Up, switch one on.

393
00:27:53,601 --> 00:27:58,600
Mapping these electrical impulses to computer commands

394
00:27:58,700 --> 00:28:02,040
allows a user to control the machine.

395
00:28:02,140 --> 00:28:05,640
For instance, a user can tell the computer a quick glance

396
00:28:05,740 --> 00:28:09,540
right moves the cursor right, and raising an eyebrow

397
00:28:09,640 --> 00:28:11,960
moves the cursor left.

398
00:28:15,060 --> 00:28:17,960
A slight tightening of the jaw raises the cursor,

399
00:28:18,060 --> 00:28:21,100
and relaxing the muscle makes the cursor fall.

400
00:28:21,101 --> 00:28:23,320
makes the cursor fall.

401
00:28:23,321 --> 00:28:26,340
Now the person has full control of a mouse.

402
00:28:26,341 --> 00:28:27,540
Take it up.

403
00:28:27,541 --> 00:28:31,120
But perhaps an even more amazing attribute of brain fingers

404
00:28:31,121 --> 00:28:33,200
is its ability to pick up and decipher

405
00:28:33,201 --> 00:28:36,540
the frequencies, or brain waves, generated by the brain.

406
00:28:41,140 --> 00:28:44,300
Brain fingers reads alpha and beta brain waves,

407
00:28:44,301 --> 00:28:48,200
which can be thought of as different states of mind.

408
00:28:48,201 --> 00:28:52,760
When relaxed, the brain produces more alpha waves.

409
00:28:52,761 --> 00:28:54,700
When the mind is mentally excited,

410
00:28:54,701 --> 00:28:59,060
alpha goes down and beta comes up.

411
00:28:59,061 --> 00:29:00,860
By mapping the electrical impulses,

412
00:29:00,861 --> 00:29:03,900
as well as the fluctuations of alpha and beta brainwaves

413
00:29:03,901 --> 00:29:06,600
to computer commands, brain fingers

414
00:29:06,601 --> 00:29:09,100
can control commercial software and games.

415
00:29:12,440 --> 00:29:15,600
Using brain fingers is as easy as slipping on a headband.

416
00:29:18,200 --> 00:29:21,360
