445
00:31:14,720 --> 00:31:16,840
A nearby cart held the components

446
00:31:16,841 --> 00:31:18,880
that would hopefully convert his brain language

447
00:31:18,881 --> 00:31:21,120
into something the computer could understand.

448
00:31:22,880 --> 00:31:25,940
When we recorded from his motor cortex

449
00:31:25,941 --> 00:31:26,880
and asked him,

450
00:31:27,620 --> 00:31:30,240
think about moving left or think about moving right,

451
00:31:30,340 --> 00:31:32,460
we saw that the brain activity changed.

452
00:31:32,840 --> 00:31:35,060
So this was a fundamental step.

453
00:31:35,360 --> 00:31:38,880
When we saw that, we knew that we could decode the information,

454
00:31:39,220 --> 00:31:41,100
interpret it as if it were hand motion,

455
00:31:41,101 --> 00:31:44,540
and then allow him to use his thoughts to control a computer cursor.

456
00:31:46,080 --> 00:31:53,400
Within days, Matthew could move the cursor around by just thinking left, right, up, and down.

457
00:31:53,960 --> 00:31:56,160
Next, I'm going to paint a circle.

458
00:32:01,140 --> 00:32:04,740
That's the best circle I can do, and I'm going to act it.

459
00:32:05,600 --> 00:32:09,320
The colors represent the activity changing in the various electrodes,

460
00:32:09,321 --> 00:32:17,420
And that twinkling patchwork of information is actually telling us something about the intention or the intended movement that the patient might want to make.

461
00:32:17,840 --> 00:32:20,060
Now I'm going to close the hand.

462
00:32:21,240 --> 00:32:27,580
Matthew has even demonstrated the ability to open and close a robotic hand just by thinking about it.

463
00:32:27,581 --> 00:32:30,000
Not bad, man. Not bad at all.

464
00:32:30,940 --> 00:32:32,820
The implications are mind-blowing.

465
00:32:33,440 --> 00:32:38,340
A future implant could give a spinal cord injury patient control of robotic limbs.

