495
00:34:47,320 --> 00:34:50,320
Rutan's team had built the first privately funded manned

496
00:34:50,321 --> 00:34:53,320
spacecraft to reach the edge of space.

497
00:34:56,320 --> 00:35:02,820
The flight was the second one within a week for the team, which won them the coveted Ansari

498
00:35:02,821 --> 00:35:13,920
X Prize, a $10 million trophy established to jumpstart the space tourism industry.

499
00:35:13,921 --> 00:35:24,460
Now the goal is to establish regular flights for the average person.

500
00:35:24,461 --> 00:35:28,320
flights could begin in just a few years.

501
00:35:28,321 --> 00:35:37,700
Estimated cost for a seat into space, $200,000.

502
00:35:37,701 --> 00:35:40,920
An orbiting space station luxury resort could be next.

503
00:35:47,800 --> 00:35:52,660
2004 was also a banner year for NASA.

504
00:35:52,661 --> 00:35:56,800
two Mars exploration rovers, Spirit and Opportunity,

505
00:35:56,801 --> 00:35:58,660
started exploring the red planet.

506
00:36:01,040 --> 00:36:04,220
And then there was the big announcement from President Bush.

507
00:36:04,221 --> 00:36:08,380
Today we set a new course for America's space program.

508
00:36:09,780 --> 00:36:12,220
With the experience and knowledge gained on the moon,

509
00:36:13,640 --> 00:36:15,800
we will then be ready to take the next steps

510
00:36:15,801 --> 00:36:20,120
of space exploration, human missions to Mars

511
00:36:20,121 --> 00:36:22,220
and to worlds beyond.

512
00:36:29,820 --> 00:36:33,040
The president's commitment for a manned mission to Mars

513
00:36:33,041 --> 00:36:35,760
included building a new crew exploration vehicle,

514
00:36:37,160 --> 00:36:39,660
establishing an extended human presence on the moon,

515
00:36:40,340 --> 00:36:43,340
and eventually going to Mars.

516
00:36:44,720 --> 00:36:46,980
Cost estimates ranged widely,

517
00:36:47,280 --> 00:36:49,340
from $50 to $500 billion.

518
00:36:50,120 --> 00:36:55,120
The US has been interested in the Red Planet

519
00:36:55,121 --> 00:36:56,120
for a long time.

520
00:36:58,120 --> 00:37:02,120
1964, Mariner 4 flyby.

521
00:37:06,120 --> 00:37:11,120
1975, Viking 1 and 2 missions.

522
00:37:14,120 --> 00:37:18,120
1996, Mars Pathfinder.

523
00:37:20,120 --> 00:37:27,460
2003, Mars Exploration Rovers.

524
00:37:29,960 --> 00:37:32,400
Future missions will be even more ambitious.

525
00:37:33,520 --> 00:37:36,940
There are a lot of problems to be solved, a lot of very exciting challenges,

526
00:37:37,340 --> 00:37:41,820
and this is not a time where we can arbitrarily throw out any potential answer.

527
00:37:41,821 --> 00:37:46,620
Five, four, three, two, one, we have ignition.

528
00:37:46,621 --> 00:37:50,620
One challenge is trying to reduce trip duration.

529
00:37:50,621 --> 00:37:54,620
Right now, the journey takes seven months, each way.

530
00:37:54,621 --> 00:37:59,620
Once on the red planet, time will also be an issue.

531
00:37:59,621 --> 00:38:02,620
When people are on the surface of Mars,

532
00:38:02,621 --> 00:38:05,620
every work hour is going to be a very precious commodity.

533
00:38:05,621 --> 00:38:09,620
So you certainly don't want to engage those people doing mundane things.

534
00:38:09,621 --> 00:38:13,620
One of the models would be that you actually send robots,

535
00:38:13,621 --> 00:38:17,620
and a lot of the infrastructure that people will count on

536
00:38:17,621 --> 00:38:19,620
ahead of sending the people.

537
00:38:22,620 --> 00:38:25,620
Robots could set up prefab habitats,

538
00:38:25,621 --> 00:38:27,620
even before the first crew arrives.

539
00:38:28,620 --> 00:38:31,620
Various groups of engineers, architects and dreamers

540
00:38:31,621 --> 00:38:34,620
have developed concepts for the first Martian base,

541
00:38:36,620 --> 00:38:39,620
including this one from the Sasakawa International Center

542
00:38:39,621 --> 00:38:41,620
for Space Architecture.

543
00:38:41,621 --> 00:38:47,020
Over my shoulder, we have a model of one of the Mars-based

544
00:38:47,021 --> 00:38:51,020
scenarios that we've established that has a variety of different

545
00:38:51,021 --> 00:38:52,020
kinds of elements.

546
00:38:52,021 --> 00:38:56,020
Some of the larger ones are what we call pneumatic structures.

547
00:38:56,021 --> 00:39:00,020
Upon arrival, it will deploy, it will inflate into a larger

548
00:39:00,021 --> 00:39:01,020
structure.

549
00:39:01,021 --> 00:39:05,020
We also may look at telescoping structures,

550
00:39:05,021 --> 00:39:08,020
and they extend out.

551
00:39:08,021 --> 00:39:11,020
Most designs are still on the drawing board.

552
00:39:11,021 --> 00:39:16,360
But located in a Mars-like environment in the Utah desert,

553
00:39:16,361 --> 00:39:19,900
a Martian base already exists.

554
00:39:23,100 --> 00:39:25,900
Simulations at the Mars Desert Research Station

555
00:39:25,901 --> 00:39:28,300
bring space exploration down to Earth.

556
00:39:31,540 --> 00:39:35,380
The Mars Desert Research Station, or NDRS,

557
00:39:35,840 --> 00:39:37,780
is a simulated Mars base.

558
00:39:37,781 --> 00:39:49,658
So that's basically a facility where engineers and scientists can test procedures and equipment and similar things in preparation for building the first actual Mars

559
00:39:49,659 --> 00:39:50,359
base.

560
00:39:52,400 --> 00:40:03,050
The MDRS was established by the Mars Society, a private organization of specialists and researchers whose goals include promoting the manned exploration and settlement

561
00:40:03,051 --> 00:40:03,751
of Mars.

562
00:40:07,780 --> 00:40:12,260
Volunteers spend two-week rotations here.

563
00:40:12,261 --> 00:40:15,620
Crew 37 happens to be from Georgia Tech.

564
00:40:15,621 --> 00:40:18,420
The 11, I'm sure you guys do these to the HAB.

565
00:40:18,421 --> 00:40:21,300
The purpose of MDRS is to gather data

566
00:40:21,301 --> 00:40:24,020
and to gather experience to collect lessons learned

567
00:40:24,021 --> 00:40:29,060
on how to operate and design such a planetary surface base.

568
00:40:29,061 --> 00:40:31,980
So there are many elements that you could theoretically simulate

569
00:40:31,981 --> 00:40:35,580
on a computer or as a separate part in a lab.

570
00:40:35,581 --> 00:40:38,260
But as soon as you combine everything with the human crew,

571
00:40:38,720 --> 00:40:41,040
that adds a whole other layer of complexity.

572
00:40:41,820 --> 00:40:45,080
And this is why it's so important to put everything together in one place,

573
00:40:45,220 --> 00:40:47,800
then add a crew, and then see how it works.

574
00:40:47,960 --> 00:40:52,180
Because the whole purpose of sending a crew to Mars is to have them be productive.

575
00:40:56,680 --> 00:40:57,460
That's right.

576
00:40:57,940 --> 00:41:00,240
A trip to Mars won't actually be a vacation.

577
00:41:01,760 --> 00:41:05,100
The crew will be busy building, exploring, and surviving.

578
00:41:05,580 --> 00:41:13,360
Because transportation costs are so high, the goal is to pack light and use as many

579
00:41:13,361 --> 00:41:17,820
Martian natural resources as possible.

580
00:41:17,821 --> 00:41:20,260
Oxygen can be generated from the Martian atmosphere.

581
00:41:20,261 --> 00:41:26,320
The Martian atmosphere consists of mainly carbon dioxide, CO2, so with the rather straightforward

582
00:41:26,321 --> 00:41:31,340
chemical process, if you have enough power, you can extract the O2 from the CO2.

583
00:41:31,341 --> 00:41:36,340
The thin Martian atmosphere is 95% carbon dioxide.

584
00:41:36,341 --> 00:41:39,340
Pioneer Astronautics, under contract with NASA,

585
00:41:39,341 --> 00:41:43,340
has already designed a solar-powered device that breaks down CO2

586
00:41:43,341 --> 00:41:46,340
into carbon monoxide and oxygen.

587
00:41:46,341 --> 00:41:49,340
Producing water is a little trickier.

588
00:41:49,341 --> 00:41:53,340
Hydrogen, the primary ingredient of H2O, is hard to come by.

589
00:41:53,341 --> 00:41:59,340
However, there are indications that Mars was once partially covered with water.

590
00:41:59,341 --> 00:42:06,340
Maybe subsurface ice still exists and can be melted for human use.

591
00:42:06,341 --> 00:42:09,340
Finding water is important for another reason.

592
00:42:09,341 --> 00:42:11,340
Was there water? Is there water?

593
00:42:11,341 --> 00:42:13,340
If there is water, where is the water?

594
00:42:13,341 --> 00:42:17,340
And why the water is important is that often, you know,

595
00:42:17,341 --> 00:42:22,340
it's felt that where there is water, there is some potential for life.

596
00:42:22,341 --> 00:42:25,340
And that, of course, is, I think, the ultimate question

597
00:42:25,341 --> 00:42:27,340
that people are intrigued by.

598
00:42:27,341 --> 00:42:30,340
intrigued by, you know, is there life out there?

599
00:42:33,340 --> 00:42:36,140
Robots can only do so much.

600
00:42:36,141 --> 00:42:40,020
A manned mission might be the only way to find out.

601
00:42:40,021 --> 00:42:42,980
Our civilization is currently at a level of technology

602
00:42:42,981 --> 00:42:45,860
where we can send humans to Mars.

603
00:42:45,861 --> 00:42:49,300
However, the level of technology is different from having

604
00:42:49,301 --> 00:42:52,460
the actual equipment that's needed, all the systems,

605
00:42:52,461 --> 00:42:55,860
and integrating those systems and making sure they actually work.

606
00:42:55,861 --> 00:43:00,540
So my best guess is it will take us about 10 to 15 years

607
00:43:00,541 --> 00:43:04,220
before all the systems are ready and integrated

608
00:43:04,221 --> 00:43:08,460
and we can confidently send a crew to Mars.

609
00:43:08,461 --> 00:43:11,420
That's an optimistic estimate, according to most experts.

610
00:43:16,660 --> 00:43:19,780
That's the trouble with trying to predict the future.

611
00:43:19,781 --> 00:43:21,860
It only takes time to be proven wrong.

612
00:43:21,861 --> 00:43:26,860
Forecasting the future is a strange and risky business

613
00:43:26,861 --> 00:43:28,860
because you hear people saying today,

614
00:43:28,861 --> 00:43:30,860
well, where are the flying cars?

615
00:43:30,861 --> 00:43:31,860
We don't see them.

616
00:43:31,861 --> 00:43:34,860
A lot of the things that have been forecast

617
00:43:34,861 --> 00:43:36,860
have in fact come true.

618
00:43:36,861 --> 00:43:39,860
It's just that we haven't been able to predict

619
00:43:39,861 --> 00:43:42,860
which ones of them will come true when.

620
00:43:42,861 --> 00:43:48,860
Will we be vacationing on Mars in 2060 or 2160?

621
00:43:48,861 --> 00:43:50,860
Hard to predict.

622
00:43:50,861 --> 00:43:54,360
But predicting the future isn't about exactness.

623
00:43:54,361 --> 00:43:59,460
It's about stirring imaginations, helping us avoid impending

624
00:43:59,461 --> 00:44:02,900
disaster, and making for a better tomorrow.

625
00:44:05,400 --> 00:44:09,220
Today's fantasies are tomorrow's facts.

626
00:44:09,221 --> 00:44:14,720
So the sooner we get dreaming, the sooner we'll reach the stars.

